KEYWORDS: greenhouse gas monitoring, metal−organic frameworks, quartz crystal microbalance, methane sensor, carbon dioxide sensor
R eal-time sensing of greenhouse gases is essential in ing. Fugitive methane and carbon dioxide emissions, e.g., from distribution pipelines, abandoned oil and gas wells, and carbon capture sites, have become increasingly concerning due to their effect on global warming. Therefore, real-time monitoring, quantifying, and locating emission sources are essential.1−3 Commercially available solid-state sensors offer a cost-effective solution but are hampered by poor selectivity, irreversible poisoning at saturation levels, and short lifespan under nonideal conditions. Nondispersive infrared spectroscopy sensors address some limitations but are sensitive to environmental conditions, especially humidity and rapid temperature changes.4 Thus, there is a need to develop sensor technology based on new detection mechanisms and with improved practical applicability.5,6
Quartz crystal microbalance (QCM)-based sensors are technologically simple and cost-effective with high theoretical sensitivity.7,8 The working principle is based on the piezoelectric property of quartz crystals.9 This means that they produce an electrical charge under mechanical stress. The process is reversible; thus, when an alternating voltage is applied to a quartz crystal, it oscillates at a fundamental frequency. This frequency is dependent on the thickness and mass of the quartz crystal. For use as a sensor, the quartz crystal is typically surface-functionalized with a thin film capable of binding target molecules (Figure 1). The sorption of guest molecules changes the mass, yielding a measurable reduction in frequency reduction. The relationship between frequency and mass change is linear for rigid films and described by the Sauerbrey equation.10 The theoretical mass sensitivity constant of a $10~\mathrm{MHz}$ resonator is $4.4~\mathrm{ng}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ $\mathrm{Hz^{1-}}$ .11 For application, the critical challenge is functionalization of the quartz resonator with a selective analyte-recognizing film. The film is ideally based on a material that binds the target molecule in the presence of other species with a high selectivity. Second, it can be used for enrichment and sensitivity enhancement, which is especially challenging for low-molecular-mass analytes.12
Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of extended network solids containing potential voids.13,14 MOFs are prepared by self-assembling inorganic nodes, typically polyatomic metal clusters (secondary building units or SBUs), and organic linker molecules.15 The well-defined connectivity and topology of the SBUs yield high structural predictability and control.16 In simplified terms, this allows us to create materials with specific properties ‘on-demand’ by the purposeful choice of the building blocks. This, combined with high porosity and internal surface area, makes MOFs attractive as analyte-recognizing sensor materials. For QCM sensing specifically, MOFs can be deposited as thin films on the electrode surface, e.g., via liquid-phase layer-by-layer, electrochemical or chemical vapor deposition.17,18 Metal−organic frameworks have successfully been demonstrated for detecting humidity, volatile organic compounds, and other toxic gases.19−23 However, few examples of sensing $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and/or $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ have been described.24−28 Key challenges that must be addressed, especially significant for $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ include poor response rate and detection limit, directly correlating to the selectivity toward other atmospheric gases (e.g., $\mathrm{N}_{2},\mathrm{O}_{2})$ .
Herein, we demonstrate how quartz crystal resonators functionalized with the microporous MOF [Cu(hfipbb)- $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)]$ $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{hfpbb}\ =\ 4,4^{\prime}\cdot$ -(hexafluoroisopropylidene)bis(benzoic acid)) can be used for the gravimetric sensing of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under ambient conditions. This MOF was first described by Jiang et al. in 2009.30 Structurally, the MOF is constructed from copper paddlewheel SBUs connected by twisted fluorinated dicarboxylate linkers with a carboxylate $-a-$ carboxylate angle of $110^{\circ}$ , giving an interpenetrated helical assembly with tubular channels down the $b$ axis (Figure 2). The SBU is identical to the archetypal copper paddlewheel material HKUST-1, which has been evaluated for sensing applications, including greenhouse gases.31−33 For example, Hromadka and Chong et al. described optical fibers chemically modified with an HKUST-1 thin film for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensing.34,35 However, no details about cross-sensitivity were reported. Specifically, HKUST-1 has also been used to functionalize quartz resonators for sensing plant oil odors and humidity.36,37
A key difference between the materials is that $\mathrm{[Cu(hfipb)}$ - $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)]$ has the smaller pore size and aperture (3 versus 14 Å), which can be used for size exclusion to reduce cross-sensitivity toward larger analytes as demonstrated by gas adsorption experiments.38 Furthermore, HKUST-1 is known to be waterand humidity-sensitive with degradation over time under atmospheric conditions which limits the practical applicability.39,40 S tructurally, the channels of $\left[\mathrm{\bar{C}u(h f b b)}\right]\mathrm{\bar{(H_{2}O)}]}$ contain small cavities connected by narrow windows of 2.96 Å (calculated using PoreBlazer $4.0^{4\mathrm{{\dot{1}}}}$ ). This is slightly less than the kinetic diameters of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (3.80 and $3.30\mathrm{~\AA},$ respectively).42 This slight difference yields a measurable difference in diffusion coefficients, which may be exploited for analyte discrimination as described later. For device integration, we fabricated thin films on the gold surface of the QCM resonator using a sequential deposition protocol. The first step includes the formation of a carboxylate-terminated selfassembly monolayer (SAM), which is used to bind an initial layer of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions, forming the basis for the continued MOF growth. The deposition method was chosen to prepare mechanically robust films, providing continuous QCM measurements. The sensor shows a rapid response to both $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Regarding practical applicability, the sensor is fully reversible even at saturation (i.e., $100\%$ gas levels) and shows no performance loss when stored under normal humid conditions for several months. More interestingly, by analyzing overtones, it is possible to discriminate whether the response is due to the adsorption of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ or $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ providing a built-in selectivity factor.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Reagents and Materials. All materials and reagents were sourced commercially and used without further purification. Absolute ethanol, 16-mercaptohexadecanoic acid (16-MHDA, ${>}90\%$ ), copper(II) acetate monohydrate $\left(>98\%\right).$ , and acetic acid $(100\%)$ were purchased from Merck KGaA, Germany. $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -(Hexafluoroisopropylidene)bis(benzoic acid) $(>98\%)$ was purchased from TCI Europe NV, Belgium. AT-cut piezoelectric quartz crystal resonators $\left(10\ \mathrm{\MHz},\right.$ gold electrodes $6\ \mathrm{mm}$ diameter) were purchased from Novaetech S.r.l., Italy. Oxygen $\left(>99.995\%\right)$ , methane $\left(>99.5\%\right)$ , and carbon dioxide $\left(>99.99\%\right)$ were purchased from Air Liquide $\mathbf{A}/S$ , Denmark. Nitrogen $(99.999\%)$ was supplied from a Nano GEN2 PSA generator.
Synthesis of $\mathsf{I C u}(\mathsf{h f p b}\bar{\mathsf{b}}\mathsf{b})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})]$ . A slightly modified literature procedure was used to prepare the MOF in bulk.30 $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}$ $\mathrm{\DeltaH}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\left(73.3~\mathrm{mg},0.36~\mathrm{mmol}\right)$ and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -(hexafluoroisopropylidene)bis(benzoic acid) $\cdot144.1\mathrm{~mg},0.36\mathrm{~mmol})$ were added to a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ glass pressure vessel containing $15~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The vial was sealed with a crimp cap and sonicated for $15\mathrm{min}$ yielding a suspension of the linker in aqueous copper acetate. The reaction mixture was heated to $125^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 days, after which a fine crystalline blue powder precipitated. The powder was isolated by filtration and washed by sonication in acetone $(10~\mathrm{mL})$ ), followed by ethanol $\mathrm{\Omega}^{\mathrm{'}10\ m L)}$ to remove excess linker. The cleaned material was isolated by filtration and air-dried, leaving $\left[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{hfbb})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\right]$ as a light blue powder $(132\mathrm{~mg,~}0.28\mathrm{~mmol,}$ $78\%$ ). The as-synthesized MOF was heated at $140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in an oven for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ to remove trapped solvent molecules (hereafter referred to as “activated $\scriptstyle\mathrm{MOF}^{\prime\prime}$ ). IR (FT-ATR diamond anvil, recorded on a Bruker $\alpha$ II with resolution $2~{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ ) $\nu/{\mathrm{cm}^{-1}}$ IR $\left(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\right)$ : 3674(b), 3419(b), 1609(s), $1559(\mathrm{m})$ , 1402(vs), 1324(w), $1292(\mathrm{m})$ , 1257(s), 1237(s), 1210(s), 1197 $\mathbf{\tau}(\mathbf{m})$ , 1169(vs), 1136(s), 1022(w), $974(\mathrm{m})$ , $962(\mathrm{m})$ , $947(\mathrm{m})$ , 928(m), $860(\mathrm{m})$ , $846(\mathrm{m})$ , 780(s), 749(s), 723(vs), 692(w), $556(\mathrm{w}),532(\mathrm{m}),515(\mathrm{s}),495(\mathrm{s})$ ).
Characterization of Sensor Films. Scanning electron and atomic force microscopy measurements were performed using an FEI Quanta 650F and Bruker Dimension Icon AFM (tapping mode, TAP150Ai-G probe), respectively. The structural identity of the film was confirmed by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) on a Malvern Panalytical Empyrean diffractometer equipped with a Bragg− Brentano high-definition incident monochromator and a 1Der detector using Cu Kα (1.5406 Å) radiation in reflection mode.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Sensor Fabrication and Characterization. The MOF functionalized quartz crystal resonators (hereafter referred to as sensors) were prepared by layer-by-layer epitaxial growth by using a custom-designed flow setup. The film deposition cell allowed us to limit exposure of the growth solutions to the gold electrode, avoiding the deposition of crystals on the surrounding quartz surface and the contacts. Before the MOF growth, a SAM of 16-mercaptohexadecanoic acid was deposited on the gold surface. The SAM acts as a binder for an initial layer of copper atoms to grow the film sequentially. Thus, the first layer of the film is covalently bonded to the gold electrode, providing mechanical and chemical strength. This is important for the sensor’s practical applicability and measurement stability. A quartz crystal microbalance is highly sensitive to minor disturbances; therefore, the sensing film must be robust, reducing the risk of particle movement and dislodgement. In our experience, ex situ methods, e.g., drop-casting using solvent suspensions of powdered MOF, are useful for short-term testing but are not used for continuing use. Furthermore, they are associated with variable surface coverage (i.e., coffee ring effect), an important factor in QCM due to the resonators’ nonuniform mass sensitivity distribution.44,45 The structural identity of the film was confirmed by powder X-ray diffraction (Figure 3). The thin-film powder pattern matches the bulk material and theoretical values calculated from the single-crystal structure deposited in the Cambridge Structural Database (CSD reference code VUKHEP).30 With a carboxylate-terminated SAM, $\left[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{hfbb})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\right]$ has two preferred growth planes, [102] and [010] (Figure S4). Scanning electron microscopy imaging shows island growth of tabular/disk-like crystallites with a long edge pointing toward the surface (Figure 4). Calculation of the morphology according to the Bravais−Friedel−Donnay−Harker law (using CCDC Mercury 2020.20.0) shows that the material forms monoclinic prism-shaped crystals. The disk-like crystals thus indicate anisotropic growth where the [010] plane grows more rapidly. Although this suggests an oriented film, where the onedimensional (1D) channels are perpendicular to the sensor surface, the PXRD peak intensity ratios of the bulk material compared to the thin film do not indicate a preferred orientation. The number of deposition cycles was used to control the thickness of the film. For 25 cycles, a thin film with an approximate mass of $29\mu\mathrm{g}$ was deposited (calculated using the Sauerbrey equation, assuming that the film is rigid under standard conditions). Increasing the number of cycles to 120 resulted in a film with an approximate mass of $87\mu\mathrm{g}.$ Furthermore, the concentration of the inorganic and organic precursors can be used to control the crystallite size distribution, with lower concentrations yielding a more finely structured film. The cost is the more extended number of cycles to reach the same mass deposition, increasing the overall time of sensor preparation. The reproducibility of the deposition method was tested by repeat film growth on three different resonators using the low concentration method, yielding films with masses of 9.7, 8.7, and 6.5 (standard deviation $1.6\ \mu\mathrm{g})$ . We believe that the main source of the variability is the loss of elasticity of the peristaltic tubing due to solvent and reagent exposure, leading to inconsistent flow rates over time. Scanning electron microscopy of a cross section of the thin film of $87~\mu\mathrm{g}$ shows an average thickness of $2.5\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ (Figure S5). Based on the density obtained from single-crystal XRD data $\mathrm{i.707~g/cm^{3}}$ (disregarding bulk crystal density), the calculated thickness is $1.8\mu\mathrm{m},$ which is in good agreement with the SEM-derived thickness. The difference is attributed to voids at the interaggregate grain boundaries, also observed in AFM imaging. For thicker films, both the third and fifth overtones were dampened to the extent that they could not be measured. The overtones (resonance excited harmonics) provide information on film rigidity and increased mass sensitivity (at the cost of signal-to-noise ratio).9 Thus, the lack of overtones reduces the amount of information about the system that can be determined. Therefore, only thinner films were used for the sensing studies.
Sensor Performance. Due to the explosive hazard of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ mixtures, the primary calibration and adsorption study was carried out with gases diluted in pure $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ . Sensor response at various concentrations was evaluated (Figures 5 and 6 and Table 1) for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Due to the microporous structure, the material is expected to exhibit monolayer adsorption at low pressure. The response $(\Delta f_{\mathrm{i}},$ defined as the change in frequency at equilibrium in analyte gas flow compared with the preceding $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ flow) follows what can be best described as an IUPAC type I isotherm. The data were further analyzed (under the simplified assumption that the Sauerbrey equation is valid under the experimental conditions) using the Python General Adsorption Processing Suite (pyGAPS), which showed the best fit against a Langmuir-type adsorption isotherm (Figures S6 and S7).46 The precision of the sensor calibration was tested by measuring the response toward both analytes from 2 to $100\%\ \mathrm{(v/v)}$ using three separate sensors of similar mass deposition (Figures S8 to S10). The sensors show good reproducibility, with a slightly higher standard error toward $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ which is believed to result from an overall lower response and, thus, a higher signal-to-noise ratio.
The film shows a noticeable change in dissipation response, indicating a viscoelastic behavior due to the adsorption process.47 Change in dissipation response arises due to a change in viscoelastic behavior of a thin film of fluid at the interface of the MOF-gas stream. Quantifying the viscoelastic behavior needs considerations of the viscosities and densities of the fluids/fluid mixtures and the smoothness of the quartz film. Moreover, it only applies to higher overtones, and the behavior deviates from the model for the fundamental frequency.48 In our case, we see only a dissipation response in the fundamental frequency. We attribute this to the swelling of the framework due to the size discrepancy between guest molecules and 1D channels, i.e., the channel windows must expand to accommodate the guest molecules. Although $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ has a smaller kinetic diameter, the dissipation response is higher than $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . This is assumed to be due to an overall higher molecular uptake due to stronger interactions with the framework.
The MOF contains binuclear copper nodes with four carboxylate linkers binding to the metal site in the bridging mode (i.e., paddlewheel structure). The binding of a water molecule saturates the coordination sphere of each copper atom. The water molecules can be removed by thermal activation, and it is known that open metal sites may increase the binding affinity toward $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .49,50 However, the material will, over time, readsorb water molecules from the atmosphere (which can be visually corroborated by a change of color from dark to light blue). To evaluate the effect of water content, response toward methane was measured for an assynthesized sensor preactivation and again postactivation for the same sensor. Subsequently, the film was saturated by submerging it in water, and the response was measured again, followed by reactivation and another measurement. A near identical response was observed toward $25\%$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ $\left(\mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v}\right)$ (Figure S11). Additionally, the effect of relative humidity on the sensor was evaluated (Figure S12) by measuring the response toward $25\%$ $\mathrm{CH_{4}}/\mathrm{CO_{2}}$ in dry synthetic ( $80{:}20~\mathrm{N}_{2}/$ $|\mathrm{O}_{2}\rangle$ , atmospheric $\mathrm{(RH38\%}$ ), and humid air $(\mathrm{RH}65\%$ , obtained from bubbling compressed air through a saturated aqueous sodium chloride solution).51 The relative humidity was verified by a hand-held sensor (RS Pro RS-172 datalogger). For $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ sensitivity is slightly decreased when moving to humid air.
Sensor Selectivity. Cross-sensitivity between $\mathrm{CH}_{4},\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in ${\bf N}_{2}$ was evaluated. The sensor is responsive to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ but shows no significant sensitivity toward $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Figure S13). The sensitivity is approximately 2.0 and $15.6~\mathrm{Hz}$ per $\mu\mathrm{g}$ of MOF film for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ respectively. First, we attribute the higher sensitivity toward $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to its slightly smaller kinetic diameter, yielding a higher penetration depth of the gas molecules within the film. Second, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ has a quadrupole moment. This favors electrostatic interaction between the aromatic $\pi$ system of the linker molecule and the electrondeficient carbon atom. In contrast, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is predominantly expected to interact via a weaker, $\pi{\mathrm{-HC}}$ interaction between the gas molecules and internal MOF surface. This is corroborated by the slope of the calibration curve in the linear region, as well as the overall mass uptake (Figures 5 and 6). Theoretical and experimental adsorption studies confirm this on a separate phase, $[\mathrm{Cu(hfpbb)(\bar{H}_{2}h f p b b)_{0.5}}],$ showing that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ has a higher heat of adsorption than $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .52,53 Structurally, both MOFs have a nearly identical internal channel structure, chemical environment, surface area, and porosity. Bao et al. calculated the diffusion coefficient of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ to be $2.97\times10^{-3}$ and $1.14\times10^{-4}\:s^{-1}$ , respectively, in the same material.54 Wu et al. determined adsorption isotherms up to 1 bar, demonstrating that the material has approximately half the molar capacity of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ compared to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ (0.45 versus $0.8\ \mathrm{mmol\g^{-1}}.$ ).53 The materials showed Langmuir (type I) isotherms typical of microporous materials. Both are in good agreement with our QCM measurements.
Interestingly, the kinetic and thermodynamic differences result in different penetration depths of both molecules within the film. For a QCM, the penetration depth of the measurement decreases with increasing overtone number as the higher frequencies probe closer to the quartz surface.55−57 Thus, the analysis of the frequency changes in the fundamental and overtone frequencies allows us to probe adsorption at different vertical regions of the MOF film. This can be exploited to discriminate between $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (Table 2 and Figures 8 and S14−S16). The frequency ratio deviations $(\bar{f_{\mathrm{ratio\_i}}}=\Delta f_{\mathrm{i}}/\Delta f_{\mathrm{l}}-\mathrm{(i))}$ were calculated based on the average sensor response of 10 consecutive sensing and regeneration cycles. For example, $10\%$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $1\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ yield equal responses at the fundamental frequency $\left(\Delta f_{1}\approx14\:\:\mathrm{Hz}\right)$ . However, at the fifth fundamental, the responses are 80 and 73 $\mathbf{Hz},$ yielding a frequency ratio deviation of 0.6 and 0.9 for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ respectively. It is worth noting that the frequency ratio deviation depends on concentration and should be calibrated equally to frequency reduction before use. Furthermore, the dissipation response for both molecules is slightly different when the fundamental frequency response is the same. This difference can be utilized to distinguish further which molecule is responsible for the sensor response and be used as additional factors/input in more comprehensive data processing methods (e.g., principal component analyses).57
Limit of Detection and Quantification. The calibration curve for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ corresponds to a type I adsorption isotherm, consisting of a linear Henry region, followed by a horizontal plateau at saturation. The partition coefficient $(k)$ describes the distribution of the guest molecules between the gas phase and the porous film. Assuming that Henry’s law is valid and that the frequency change is exclusively a result of guest molecule adsorption, the slope of the linear range in $\Delta f$ $\left(\mathrm{Hz}\right)$ versus concentration is the partitioning coefficient. Thus, a steeper calibration curve results from a higher partition coefficient, i.e., sensitivity. The slope of the linear region (Figures S17 and S18) of the calibration curve is $-0.69$ and $-17.25\ \mathrm{\Hz}$ per vol $\%$ for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ $(<10\%)$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $(<1\%)$ , respectively. For $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ the experimentally determined LOQ is $1\%$ , with an LOD of $0.4\%$ . For $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ the LOQ is $0.2\%$ (all $\mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v}$ $\%$ in $\mathrm{N}_{2}^{\cdot}$ ).
Stability and Robustness. A challenge with MOF-based sensors is stability, especially under humid conditions, where water molecules can penetrate the framework and disconnect the relatively labile metal−carboxylate bonds. Therefore, the MOF material was stress-tested in bulk by shaking it in water for 3 days. Then, samples were taken daily and characterized by powder X-ray diffraction. No crystallinity or material integrity loss was observed during this period (Figure S19). More importantly, the sensors show insignificant performance loss when stored under ambient and uncontrolled conditions for up to 3 months (more extended period not tested), as determined by comparing the response factors of 50 and $100\%$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (Figure 9). This indicates that the sensor material is suitable for long-term applicability under standard conditions.
## CONCLUSIONS
We have demonstrated a $\mathrm{CH_{4}}/\mathrm{CO_{2}}$ sensor based on the principle of QCM with a sensor element composed of a MOF tailored for gas adsorption. The sensor was demonstrated by using low-cost and open-source electronics, facilitating system integration with significant customizability. The device is characterized by high sensitivity, selectivity, and robustness under atmospheric conditions over long periods. Most importantly, it is possible to differentiate between a $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ or $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ response by analyzing multiple harmonics and exploring frequency ratio deviations. In addition to being useful for sensing purposes, this method provides essential information regarding guest molecule transport and adsorption kinetics within porous materials and should be explored further.
Looking forward, we will focus on multivariate calibration and chemometric processing of sensor response data. First, the goal is to use advanced analyses to not only discriminate between $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ but also quantify their ratio in mixtures. Computational methods could also be complemented by adding multiple sensors with different selectivity in an array, i.e., creating an “electronic nose”.28,58 Although the sensor shows excellent metrics for industrial applications where leak detection is the primary goal, the sensor lacks the required sensitivity for environmental monitoring where $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and/or $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ are present at low ppm levels. QCM is, theoretically, sensitive enough. The limiting factor is the selectivity of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}/$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\Nu_{2}/\Nu_{2}$ . Research and development of new porous materials or composites should be invested in to improve this aspect. For MOF materials where channels propagate in one or two dimensions, the development of oriented growth protocols is warranted, as improper alignment of the channels can block the adsorption of guest molecules, reducing response rate and sensitivity.
